.TE
.SK
.H 2 "Notes"
.H 3 "Note [1]"
.B "e206 SHA1SQL5"
\(em multi-emit construction that produces three candidates per
password, capturing variants of the MySQL SQL5 hash format
including the leading
.B *
prefix and a leading-space variant:
.DS
.ft CW
s = "*" . upper(sha1(sha1_bin(pass)))
emit(sha1(s))
emit(sha1("*" . sha1(sha1_bin(pass))))
emit(sha1(" " . s))
.ft
.DE
.H 3 "Note [2]"
.B "e435 MD5SPECAM"
\(em a parsing wrapper, not a distinct hash primitive.
The input record is colon-separated of the form
.DS
.ft CW
username:email@domain[:password]
.ft
.DE
The wrapper lower-cases each field and extracts:
.DS
.ft CW
user  = lower( field 0 of input )
email = lower( field 1 of input )         # the "name@host" part
cand  = lower( field 2 of input ) if present
cand  = user                              # otherwise
.ft
.DE
It then computes
.B both
of the following, emitting both hashes for the parsed
candidate:
.DS
.ft CW
emit(md5(user . "::" . cand))                  # = e432 MD5AM
emit(md5(user . "::" . cand . email . ":73@^bhhs&#@&^@8@*$"))   # = e433 MD5AM2
.ft
.DE
The
.B salt
position in
.B MD5AM
is the parsed user; the
.B salt2
position in
.B MD5AM2
is the parsed email.
What distinguishes
.B MD5SPECAM
from a direct invocation of
.B MD5AM
or
.B MD5AM2
is the parsing of the input record \(em the hashing itself
reuses the e432 and e433 expressions.
.H 3 "Note [3]"
.B "e969 AWSSIGV4"
\(em AWS Signature Version 4 signing-key derivation, a
four-step HMAC-SHA256 chain that mixes the password with
the request date, region, and service name to produce a
key that is then used to sign HTTP requests:
.DS
.ft CW
k1 = hmac_sha256("AWS4" . pass, date)
k2 = hmac_sha256(k1, region)
k3 = hmac_sha256(k2, service)
sk = hmac_sha256(k3, "aws4_request")
.ft
.DE
.P
The final signing key
.B sk
is what is verified.
.H 3 "Note [4]"
.B "e445 SHA1-SALT-SPECIAL"
\(em a 10-round iterated SHA-1 over a dash-delimited frame.
The initial input wraps the salt in two dashes on each side
and pads the password with two dashes on each side; each
subsequent round inserts the previous SHA-1 hex digest into
the same frame, separated from the salt and the password by
two dashes.
.DS
.ft CW
h = sha1("--".salt."----".pass."----")
for i = 1 to 9 {
    h = sha1("--".salt."--".h."--".pass."----")
}
emit(h)
.ft
.DE
The hashing primitive is plain SHA-1; what is unusual is the
buffer framing and the per-round substitution of the previous
hex digest between the two salt-dashes and the two
password-dashes.
.H 3 "Note [5]"
.B "e455 PHPBB3"
\(em the standard phpass construction used by phpBB3,
WordPress, and Drupal 7.
The MD5 chain operates on raw binary digests (not hex),
hence the use of
.B md5_bin .
The final 16-byte digest is encoded with the phpass
itoa64 alphabet via
.B phpass_encode .
.DS
.ft CW
h = md5_bin(salt . pass)
for i = 1 to 2047 { h = md5_bin(h . pass) }
"$H$9" . salt . phpass_encode(h)
.ft
.DE
The
.B "$H$9"
prefix encodes the variant ($H$) and the iteration-count
exponent (9 \(rA 2^11 = 2048 rounds).
The salt is appended literally; the phpass-encoded digest
follows.
.H 3 "Note [6]"
.B "e544 CRYPTEXT"
\(em SHA-1 of the password, with the resulting 20 bytes
byte-swapped within each 32-bit group, the literal string
"Cryptext" appended, then SHA-1 again and a final per-group
byte-swap.
There is no special hashing primitive at work \(em the
construction is fully expressible with
.B sha1_bin
and
.B cut :
.DS
.ft CW
b = sha1_bin(pass)
sw = ""
for j = 0 to 4 {
    sw = sw . cut(b, j*4+3, 1) . cut(b, j*4+2, 1)
            . cut(b, j*4+1, 1) . cut(b, j*4,   1)
}
b2 = sha1_bin(sw . "Cryptext")
out = ""
for j = 0 to 4 {
    out = out . cut(b2, j*4+3, 1) . cut(b2, j*4+2, 1)
              . cut(b2, j*4+1, 1) . cut(b2, j*4,   1)
}
emit(hex(out))
.ft
.DE
The per-group byte-swap reverses byte order within each
4-byte word; equivalent to applying
.B bswap32()
to the entire SHA-1 digest:
.DS
.ft CW
hex(bswap32(sha1_bin(pass)))
.ft
.DE
.H 3 "Note [7]"
.B "e649 SHA1MD5BASE641SALT"
\(em a single-byte salt search.
The base value is computed once per password and reused for
every candidate salt byte:
.DS
.ft CW
base = md5(base64(pass))            # 32-char hex string
.ft
.DE
mdxfind then iterates a single salt byte
.B y
through the range
.BR "9 .. 127"
(119 candidate values) and computes
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(base . single_byte(y))         # 33-byte input total
.ft
.DE
emitting each result and checking it against the target.
A match is reported with the matching salt rendered as
.B "0xYY"
(for example
.BR "0x42" ).
.P
.B "Reconstructing a found hash."
Given an mdxfind result line such as
.DS
.ft CW
SHA1MD5BASE641SALTx01 13204d41af878472790a713b8f95f581b0700bcf:0x41:password
.ft
.DE
the verifying hx expression is
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(md5(base64(pass)) . fromhex(salt))
.ft
.DE
invoked with
.B \-p
.I password
and
.B \-s
.B 41
(the two hex digits of the reported salt byte;
.B 0x41
is the ASCII letter
.B A ):
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(md5(base64(pass)) . fromhex(salt))' -p password -s 41
13204d41af878472790a713b8f95f581b0700bcf
.ft
.DE
The reproduced digest matches the
.B SHA1MD5BASE641SALTx01
field from the original result, confirming the find.
.P
.B "Full per-byte loop in hx."
The complete search can be written by precomputing the 119
single-byte salts as a binary literal and indexing into it:
.DS
.ft CW
bytes = fromhex("090a0b0c0d0e0f10111213141516171819" .
                "1a1b1c1d1e1f202122232425262728292a2b2c2d2e2f" .
                "303132333435363738393a3b3c3d3e3f40414243" .
                "4445464748494a4b4c4d4e4f50515253545556575859" .
                "5a5b5c5d5e5f606162636465666768696a6b6c6d6e6f" .
                "707172737475767778797a7b7c7d7e7f")
base = md5(base64(pass))
for i = 0 to 118 { emit(sha1(base . cut(bytes, i, 1))) }
.ft
.DE
With
.B \-i N
the per-salt SHA-1 chain extends to
.B N
rounds, hex-encoding and re-hashing between rounds.
.H 3 "Note [8]"
.B "e731 SHA1MD5UC1LC"
\(em SHA-1 of the uppercase MD5 hex string, with exactly
one of the alphabetic positions
.RB ( A ne F )
lowercased.
The 32-character MD5 hex contains some number of
alphabetic characters
.RI ( N ),
and the type emits
.I N
candidate hashes per password \(em one for each
A\(enF position taken in turn.
Hex digits 0\(en9 are unaffected by case and are skipped.
The mdxfind output line carries the matching SHA-1 digest
and the password but
.B does not
record which position was lowercased; reconstruction therefore
re-runs the full per-position search and identifies the
matching variant by matching SHA-1 against the target.
.P
The hx expression demonstrates the language's
.B for ,
.B if ,
and string-slice features:
.DS
.ft CW
b = upper(md5(pass));
for y = 0 to 31 {
    c = cut(b, y, 1);
    if lower(c) != c {
        v = cut(b, 0, y) . lower(c) .
            cut(b, add(y, 1), sub(31, y));
        emit(sha1(v))
    }
}
.ft
.DE
.B "Worked example."
For the password
.B password ,
\f(CWupper(md5(pass))\fP is
.br
\f(CW5F4DCC3B5AA765D61D8327DEB882CF99\fP
.br
which contains
14 alphabetic positions (positions 1, 3, 4, 5, 7, 9, 10, 14,
17, 22, 23, 24, 28, 29).
Running the expression above against
.B \-p
.B password
emits these 14 SHA-1 digests, in order:
.DS
.ft CW
8230320f88f5f6e5746f7511cc1c3abd5ce437ed
0867ad7acd326a09c9e3f99c88c1f524dc4cef8b
d9c9670782f1d9781d991b6b1a7e4f78e5062571
8d75ebd9d8795964754b6e0a5421463c5ab2a17d
e724c34add8b6d76072ea683b3b0f771851882e6
35285b8778ec8ee97db5b9587bcec5d6e780a809
ab8e1e6b77f59957c6b9db40cb5fe6adc00233bd
b4962ea3571a518cea8e589629b8406e2d25b723
83ad12dea7e7a0965e564f4b6f1984be4afe75ff
cbc46bedfedd79aade4babdac8bd8c9fff79de63
10880623525198324bc9313c6991fad10ceac218
78eab8a2d1ad15f28791c13b960a4959c3a0971b
55964fa55355f138d027ba96438f0b725a795f8b
6571f71314f2303cd6ae3ceb71599322a31b0116
.ft
.DE
A given mdxfind find of one of these digests with the password
.B password
is verified by checking the digest against this list.
The
.B add
and
.B sub
helpers are required because hx expressions are not
arithmetic; they take an integer iteration variable and a
constant offset and return an integer.
.H 3 "Note [9]"
.B "e732 SHA1UTF7"
\(em SHA-1 of the UTF-7 encoding of the password.
The hx primitive
.B utf7()
performs the UTF-7 conversion directly via iconv.
.P
.B "mdxfind quirk."
The mdxfind compute path appends the literal character
.B X
to the password before iconv-converting to UTF-7, then drops
the trailing converted byte from the result.
This was added to work around an iconv bug where a UTF-7
escape sequence at the end of the input would not be flushed
without a following character.
mdxfind also short-circuits the SHA-1 step when the UTF-7
output is the same as the input (i.e., when the password
contains no characters that require UTF-7 escaping) on the
grounds that the result would equal a plain SHA-1 hash and
not contribute new information.
.B "Neither workaround is reproduced in hx" :
.B utf7()
in hx works correctly for trailing-escape inputs and is used
unconditionally regardless of whether the conversion changes
the input.
.P
.B "Worked example."
For the password
.BR pass@word ,
mdxfind converts to
.BR pass+AEA-word ,
SHA-1s the converted bytes, and reports
.DS
.ft CW
SHA1UTF7x01 72987265467cb8001facc60fd620442e4c5994fe:pass@word
.ft
.DE
The hx expression reproduces this directly:
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(utf7(pass))' -p 'pass@word'
72987265467cb8001facc60fd620442e4c5994fe
.ft
.DE
A password with no UTF-7-escaping characters (e.g. plain
ASCII alphanumeric) will produce a digest equal to
.BR sha1(pass) ;
the hx expression returns this naturally, while mdxfind
suppresses the result.
.H 3 "Note [10]"
.B "e763 SHA11SALTMD5SHA256"
\(em a five-variant single-byte salt search.
The base value is
.B md5(sha256(pass))
(MD5 of the SHA-256 hex of the password) and the salt is a
single byte
.I y
in the range
.BR "9 .. 127"
(skipping
.B 10 ,
which mdxfind substitutes with the space character
.BR 0x20 ).
For each
.IR y ,
mdxfind computes
.B five
SHA-1 variants over different placements of
.I y
relative to the base value, emitting all five and reporting
matches with the salt rendered as the single character
.IR y .
.P
The five variants in hx form, where
.B salt
is the single salt byte, are:
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(salt . md5(sha256(pass)))                # variant 1
sha1(md5(sha256(pass)) . salt)                # variant 2
sha1(salt . md5(sha256(pass)) . salt)         # variant 3
sha1(salt . salt . md5(sha256(pass)))         # variant 4
sha1(md5(sha256(pass)) . salt . salt)         # variant 5
.ft
.DE
.B "Worked example."
For
.BR "pass = \(lqpassword\(rq" ,
.BR "salt = \(lqA\(rq"
(byte 0x41), the five hx invocations produce:
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(salt . md5(sha256(pass)))' -p password -s A
a391f6e587080f1dd25121ce41f1d8e03c9585f7
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(md5(sha256(pass)) . salt)' -p password -s A
2ff7a3b90fa7777349049f0576aa7edbeeea8283
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(salt.md5(sha256(pass)).salt)' -p password -s A
a0d12e54500a36a09749e653aee20ff457986054
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(salt . salt . md5(sha256(pass)))' -p password -s A
d9cbf46c2950da6f8339a314bbf830810e43af90
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(md5(sha256(pass)) . salt . salt)' -p password -s A
d3ab8996254c8552ef9f82a8bd4486a603425396
.ft
.DE
matching the five mdxfind output lines for that salt byte.
.P
.B "Reconstructing a found hash."
mdxfind reports the salt as the single byte
.IR y ,
but does
.B not
record which of the five placements produced the match.
To verify a found hash, compute all five variants for the
reported salt and select the one that matches the digest in
the
.B SHA11SALTMD5SHA256
field.
.P
The full per-byte search loop in hx requires a
.B for
loop over the 119 candidate salts (with the
.B y == 10
substitution to space) and a five-fold
.B emit
inside.
mdxfind's
.B \-i N
mode extends each per-variant SHA-1 chain to
.B N
rounds, hex-encoding and re-hashing between rounds.
.H 3 "Note [11]"
.B "e770 SHA1BASE64CUSTBASE64MD5"
\(em despite the
.B CUST
in the name, the base64 encoding uses the standard RFC 4648
alphabet
.RB ( "A\(en" Z ,
.BR "a\(en" z ,
.BR "0\(en" 9 ,
.BR + ,
.BR / ,
padding with
.BR = ).
The four variants differ in two orthogonal ways: outer-format
choice (binary base64 with newline vs hex string) and
trailing-character handling (full-length vs trimmed).
.P
The base value is built from the binary MD5 of the password,
prefixed with the literal string
.B "{MD5}"
(commonly seen in LDAP and OpenLDAP password fields):
.DS
.ft CW
inner_b64 = base64(md5_bin(pass))           # e.g. X03MO1qn...mQ==
hex_form  = md5(pass)                       # e.g. 5f4dcc3b...cf99
.ft
.DE
The four mdxfind variants in hx form are:
.DS
.ft CW
# Variant 1: outer base64 of {MD5}<inner>\\n, full length
sha1(base64("{MD5}" . inner_b64 . fromhex("0a")))

# Variant 2: same as 1, with last char stripped
b = base64("{MD5}" . inner_b64 . fromhex("0a"))
sha1(cut(b, 0, sub(length(b), 1)))

# Variant 3: outer base64 of {MD5}<hex>, full length
sha1(base64("{MD5}" . hex_form))

# Variant 4: same as 3, with the two = padding chars stripped
b = base64("{MD5}" . hex_form)
sha1(cut(b, 0, sub(length(b), 2)))
.ft
.DE
.B "Worked example"
\(em for
.BR "pass = \(lqpassword\(rq" :
.DS
.ft CW
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(base64("{MD5}".base64(md5_bin(pass)).fromhex("0a")))' -p password
17f4f61432f73b94afd0c81738cd2300368dbb00
$ hashpipe -X 'b = base64("{MD5}".base64(md5_bin(pass)).fromhex("0a")); sha1(cut(b, 0, sub(length(b), 1)))' -p password
034f2e510f9fd0968cc5ced57a751522b622c129
$ hashpipe -X 'sha1(base64("{MD5}".md5(pass)))' -p password
260c19672dc393c96e1cda85290c0fafc87f7b53
$ hashpipe -X 'b = base64("{MD5}".md5(pass)); sha1(cut(b, 0, sub(length(b), 2)))' -p password
6773f3c257e06c1784e949ce16bb8deeeeec3c2d
.ft
.DE
matching the four mdxfind output lines.
.P
.B "hx string-literal note."
hx string literals do not interpret backslash escapes \(em
.B "\(dq\\\\n\(dq"
in an hx expression is the two-character sequence
backslash, n, not a newline byte.
For the literal LF (0x0a) used in variants 1 and 2, use
.B "fromhex(\(dq0a\(dq)"
to get a single-byte string.
The trailing-character truncation in variants 2 and 4 uses
.B "cut(s, 0, sub(length(s), N))"
to drop the last
.I N
characters of the string \(em a general pattern that does
not require any new mapping function.
.H 3 "Note [12]"
.B "e942 WPA-PMKID"
\(em the WPA/WPA2 PMKID derivation, used by the PMKID
attack on WPA-PSK.
The PMK is the standard PSK-mode pairwise master key,
and the PMKID is the first 16 bytes of HMAC-SHA1 over a
fixed string and the two MAC addresses.
.DS
.ft CW
PMK   = PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1(pass, essid, 4096, 32)
PMKID = HMAC-SHA1(PMK, "PMK Name" || ap_mac || sta_mac)[0..15]
.ft
.DE
This is fully expressible in hx with the existing primitives:
.DS
.ft CW
cut(hmac_sha1(pbkdf2_sha1_bin(pass, fromhex(essid), 4096, 32),
              "PMK Name" . fromhex(ap_mac) . fromhex(sta_mac)),
    0, 32)
.ft
.DE
where
.B essid ,
.B ap_mac ,
.B sta_mac
are passed as hex strings.
mdxfind reports the find as
.B "WPA*01*"
.IB pmkid * ap_mac * sta_mac * essid * .
.H 3 "Note [13]"
.B "e943 WPA-EAPOL"
\(em the WPA/WPA2 4-way-handshake MIC.
Like PMKID, the PMK is the standard PSK-mode key.
The PTK derivation builds a 100-byte
.I PKE
buffer from the literal label
.B "Pairwise key expansion" ,
the lexicographically-ordered MACs and nonces, and an iterative
PRF expansion based on HMAC-SHA1.
The first 16 (WPA1, KCK) or 16 (WPA2, KCK) bytes of the PRF
output are the
.I KCK ,
which is then used as the key in either HMAC-MD5 (key version 1,
WPA-TKIP) or HMAC-SHA1 (key version 2, WPA-CCMP) over the EAPOL
frame to produce the MIC.
.DS
.ft CW
PMK = PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1(pass, essid, 4096, 32)

PKE = "Pairwise key expansion"     /* 23 bytes */
    || min(ap_mac, sta_mac)        /*  6 bytes */
    || max(ap_mac, sta_mac)        /*  6 bytes */
    || min(ap_nonce, sta_nonce)    /* 32 bytes */
    || max(ap_nonce, sta_nonce)    /* 32 bytes */
    || 0x00                        /*  1 byte counter */
                                   /* 100 bytes total */

/* IEEE 802.11i PRF-512 expands KCK from PMK */
KCK = HMAC-SHA1(PMK, PKE)[0..15]

if keyver == 1:        /* WPA1 / TKIP */
    MIC = HMAC-MD5(KCK, eapol)[0..15]
elif keyver == 2:      /* WPA2 / CCMP-AES */
    MIC = HMAC-SHA1(KCK, eapol)[0..15]
.ft
.DE
The
.B eapol
buffer is the 802.1X EAPOL-Key frame from message-2 of the
4-way handshake with the MIC field zeroed out.
hashcat presents the data in mode-22000 format
.RB ( WPA *
.IB keyver * MIC * mac_ap * mac_sta * essid * anonce * eapol *** ).
.P
The conditional MIC primitive can be expressed in hx with an
.B if
on the integer
.B keyver
variable, but PKE-frame construction requires the lexicographic
.B "min/max"
ordering of MAC pairs and nonces, which is straightforward with
the existing
.B <
.B >
relops once the inputs are bound.
For verification of mdxfind output, working byte-for-byte against
the hashcat 22000 reference data is recommended.
.H 3 "Note [14]"
.B "e945 APFS"
\(em an Apple File System volume key check.
The on-disk format
.RB ( $fvde$2$16$ salt $ iter $ wkey )
encodes a 16-byte salt, an iteration count, and a 40-byte
AES-wrapped 32-byte volume key.
A password is correct iff the unwrap succeeds, which is detected
by the standard RFC 3394 IV check
.RB ( 0xa6a6a6a6a6a6a6a6 ).
.DS
.ft CW
DK  = PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256(pass, salt, iter, 32)  /* 32-byte AES-256 KEK */
VOL = AES-Key-Unwrap(DK, wkey)                  /* 32 bytes on success,
                                                   empty on IV mismatch */
.ft
.DE
The hx primitive
.B aes_unwrap(kek, wrapped)
implements RFC 3394 directly: it accepts 16-byte (AES-128) or
32-byte (AES-256) KEKs and any
.IB 8 + 8 n
wrapped-input length, returning the
.I 8n -byte
plaintext on a successful IV match or the empty string on
failure.
For verification it is sufficient to test
.BR "length(aes_unwrap(\(rs..., wkey)) > 0" ;
the unwrapped volume key itself is not the published hash and
is not normally compared.
See
.B mdxfind.c
JOB_APFS for the reference implementation.
.H 3 "Note [15]"
.B "e973 VEEAM-VBK"
\(em a Veeam backup-archive password check.
The on-disk format is
.BR $vbk$* salt128hex * iter * ct32hex .
The password is encoded as UTF-16LE, run through PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1
to produce a 48-byte derived key, and the AES-CBC ciphertext is
decrypted with the first 32 bytes as the AES-256 key and the next
16 bytes as the IV.
A correct password leaves the decrypted block in the form
.IB plaintext4 || PKCS7 12 ,
i.e.\ the last twelve bytes are all
.BR 0x0c .
.DS
.ft CW
DK  = PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1(UTF-16LE(pass), salt, iter, 48)
key = DK[0..31]                  /* AES-256 key */
iv  = DK[32..47]                 /* CBC IV       */
PT  = AES-CBC-Decrypt(key, iv, ct)            /* 16 bytes */
ok  = (PT[4..15] == 0x0c \(mu 12)
.ft
.DE
The hx primitive
.B aes_cbc_decrypt(key, iv, ct)
returns the raw decrypted bytes (no padding strip); use the
.B _bin
suffix when chaining.
A canonical hx verification is
.DS
.ft CW
dk = pbkdf2_sha1_bin(utf16le(pass), fromhex(salt), iter, 48);
pt = aes_cbc_decrypt_bin(cut(dk,0,32), cut(dk,32,16), fromhex(ct));
if cut(pt,4,12) == fromhex("0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c0c") { emit("ok") }
.ft
.DE
which prints
.B ok
when the password is correct and nothing otherwise.
See
.B mdxfind.c
JOB_VEEAM_VBK for the reference implementation.
.H 3 "Note [16]"
.B "e521 SHA1SALTCX"
\(em iterated SHA-1 with double-dash delimiters.
Each round emits a separate hash:
.DS
.ft CW
h = sha1("--" . salt . "----" . pass . "----")
emit(h)
for i = 1 to N-1 {
    h = sha1("--" . salt . "--" . h . "--" . pass . "----")
    emit(h)
}
.ft R
.DE
where N is the iteration count from the hash format.
.H 3 "Note [17]"
.B "e537 PHPBB3MD5"
\(em iterated MD5 with phpass-style encoding:
.DS
.ft CW
h = md5_bin(salt . md5(pass))
for i = 1 to 2047 {
    h = md5_bin(h . md5(pass))
}
result = phpass_encode(h)
.ft R
.DE
.H 3 "Note [18]"
.B "e929 RACF-KDFAES"
\(em IBM RACF KDFAES password hash.
.DS
.ft CW
racf_kdfaes(pass, user, fromhex(salt2), fromhex(salt))
.ft R
.DE
where
.B salt
is 16 bytes (32 hex) and
.B salt2
is the 16-byte header block.
.H 3 "Note [19]"
.B "e930 TACACS"
\(em TACACS+ authentication response:
.DS
.ft CW
hex(cut(xor(md5_bin(fromhex(session) . pass
    . fromhex(seq)), fromhex("010000000000")), 0, 6))
.ft R
.DE
Generates a 6-byte ciphertext from session ID,
password, and sequence number.
.H 3 "Note [20]"
.B "e944 ANSIBLE-VAULT"
\(em Ansible Vault key derivation:
.DS
.ft CW
pbkdf2_sha256(pass, salt, 10000, 80)
.ft R
.DE
The 80-byte derived key is split: 32 bytes AES key +
32 bytes HMAC key + 16 bytes IV.
.H 3 "Note [21]"
.B "e946 OTM-SHA256"
\(em Oracle Transportation Management iterated SHA-256:
.DS
.ft CW
h = sha256_bin(salt . pass)
for i = 1 to iter-1 { h = sha256_bin(h) }
result = base64(h)
.ft R
.DE
Iteration count and salt are taken from the
.B otm_sha256:iter:salt:hash
format.
.H 3 "Note [22]"
.B "e949 SOLARWINDS"
\(em SolarWinds Orion v0:
.DS
.ft CW
base64(sha512_bin(pbkdf2_sha1_bin(pass,
    cut(lower(salt) . "1244352345234", 0, 8),
    1000, 1024)))
.ft R
.DE
The salt is the username, lowercased and right-padded
to 8 bytes with the literal string "1244352345234".
.H 3 "Note [23]"
.B "e950 SOLARWINDS2"
\(em SolarWinds Orion v1:
.DS
.ft CW
base64(sha512_bin(pbkdf2_sha1_bin(pass,
    frombase64(salt), 1000, 1024)))
.ft R
.DE
The salt is 16 random bytes, base64-encoded in the
on-disk
.B $solarwinds$1$salt$hash
format.
.H 3 "Note [24]"
.B "Multi-emit families."
Several mdxfind hash types emit
.B more than one digest
per input password rather than the single canonical
expression shown in their type row.
The Appendix A expression captures the primary form;
this note enumerates the additional digests mdxfind
also computes and checks against the target table for
each input.
.P
.B "Colon-variant emit (PASS suffix)."
The types
.BR e123 " (MD5MD5PASS),"
.BR e201 " (MD5PASSMD5),"
.BR e202 " (MD5-DBL-PASS),"
and
.BR e663 " (SHA1MD5MD5PASS)"
each emit
.B both
.DS
.ft CW
H(hex32(md5(pass)) . pass)
H(hex32(md5(pass)) . ":" . pass)
.ft
.DE
where
.B H
is the outer hash.
The colon variant captures real-world hashes where the
implementation separates the inner digest from the
password with a literal
.BR : .
The e202 and e663 rows already use
.B emit()
syntax in Appendix A; e123 and e201 do not, and the
multi-emit semantics there are implicit.
.P
.B "Colon-variant emit (USER suffix)."
The types
.BR e286 " (MD5MD5USER),"
.BR e308 " (SHA1MD5USER),"
.BR e354 " (MD5CAPMD5USER),"
and
.BR e355 " (MD5CAPMD5MD5USER)"
similarly emit
.B both
.B "H(hex32(md5(pass)) . user)"
and
.BR "H(hex32(md5(pass)) . \(dq:\(dq . user)" .
The
.B user
slot is iterated against the
.B \-u
user list (or the salt list, depending on options).
.P
.B "Single-byte salt search with colon variants."
The HEXSALT family
.RB ( e183 " MD5HEXSALT,"
.B e184
SHA1HEXSALT,
.B e185
SHA256HEXSALT,
.B e186
GOSTHEXSALT,
.B e187
HAV128HEXSALT)
iterates a single 2-hex-digit salt across all 256
candidate bytes and, for each candidate, emits
.B three
digests:
.DS
.ft CW
H(hex32(md5(pass)) . hex32(salt) . ":")
H(hex32(md5(pass)) . hex32(salt))
H(hex32(md5(pass)) . hex32(salt) . ":" . hex32(salt))
.ft
.DE
The matching salt is reported as a 2-hex-digit value in
the output.
.P
The
.B e687
.RB ( SHA1SHA1MD5MD5PASS1SALT )
and
.B e649
.RB ( SHA1MD5BASE641SALT ,
already covered by Note [7])
types also perform a 1-byte salt search and emit
multiple candidate digests per salt.
.P
.B "Truncation-length search emit (TRUNC suffix)."
The TRUNC family
.RB ( e622 " SHA1SHA256TRUNC,"
.B e626
SHA1SHA1TRUNC,
.B e636
SHA1SHA512TRUNC,
.B e644
SHA1MD6TRUNC,
.B e656
SHA1SHA1CAPTRUNC,
.B e661
SHA1SHA1UCTRUNC,
.B e678
SHA1SHA256TRUNCSALT,
.B e689
SHA1SHA512UCTRUNC,
.B e690
SHA1SALTSHA512UCTRUNC,
.B e691
SHA1SALTSHA256UCTRUNC,
.B e745
SHA1SHA384TRUNC,
.B e746
SHA1RMD160TRUNC,
.B e750
SHA1SHA1SHA1TRUNC,
.B e755
SHA1SHA1TRUNCMD5,
and related)
sweeps a truncation length
.B N
across a range
(typically 21\(en40 or 21\(en64) and for each
.B N
emits
.B two
digests \(em one truncating the inner digest from the
.B left
(taking the first
.B N
bytes) and one truncating from the
.B right
(taking the last
.B N
bytes).
The Appendix A expression shows the left-truncation form
only; the right-truncation form is also checked.
The reported salt position carries the matching
.B N
as a decimal string.
.P
.B "Multi-salt-placement emit (DSALT suffix)."
The types
.B e590
.RB ( SHA1MD5DSALT )
and
.B e591
.RB ( SHA1MD5MD5DSALT )
combine a multi-byte salt with a single-byte salt
search.
For each candidate multi-byte salt
.BR s ,
mdxfind emits four colon-aware variants:
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(md5(pass . s) . ":" . s)
sha1(md5(pass . s) . s)
sha1(md5(pass . s) . s . s)        \" no colon, doubled salt
sha1(md5(pass . s) . ":" . s . s)  \" colon, doubled salt
.ft
.DE
then iterates a fifth variant over 119 single-byte salt
values and emits an additional digest per byte.
.P
.B "Multi-form SQL5 family (SHA1SQL5, SHA1SQL5MD5, etc.)."
.B e342
.RB ( MD5SQL5-32 )
belongs to this family too: it renders the inner digest in both upper and
lower case and emits eight digests per password, over each case with and
without the leading
.B 1
and
.B *
characters.
.P
The SHA1SQL5 multi-emit construction is documented in
Note [1].
The related types
.B e712
.RB ( SHA1MD5SQL5 ),
.B e713
.RB ( SHA1MD5MD5SQL5 ),
.B e760
.RB ( SHA1SQL5MD5 ),
and
.B e761
.RB ( SHA1SQL5MD5MD5 )
share the same three-variant SQL5 emit pattern,
applied after one or more MD5 wrappings of the
password.
.P
.B "Iterated salt-set emit (MD52SALTMD5 family)."
The
.B e283
.RB ( MD52SALTMD5 ),
.B e348
.RB ( MD52SALTMD5MD5 ),
and
.B e349
.RB ( MD52SALTMD5MD5MD5 )
types iterate
.B i
and
.B j
each through the printable byte range 9\(en127 and for
each
.RB ( i ", " j )
pair emit three salt-placement variants of the digest
.RB ( "i j" " prefix,"
.RB "raw" " unprefixed,"
.RB "and" " suffix variants)."
.P
.B "Base64-RAW padding-variant emit."
The
.B e237
.RB ( MD5BASE64MD5RAW )
type, its UC counterpart
.B e375
.RB ( MD5UCBASE64MD5RAW ),
and the three composites that share its code path \(em
.B e242
.RB ( MD5BASE64MD5RAWSHA1 ),
.B e243
.RB ( MD5BASE64MD5RAWMD5 )
and
.B e244
.RB ( MD5BASE64MD5RAWMD5MD5 )
\(em emit
.B both
the full-length base64 string and a 2-character-shorter
variant
(the base64 encoding without trailing
.B ==
padding) per password.
.P
The SHA1RAW pair
.RB ( e238 " MD5BASE64SHA1RAW,"
.B e376
MD5UCBASE64SHA1RAW)
emit
.B once
each and are not part of this family. The variant exists because an md5
digest is 16 bytes, whose base64 encoding ends in two
.B =
characters; a sha1 digest is 20 bytes and its base64 encoding ends in one,
so there is no two-character variant to take.
.P
.B "PHPBB3 colon-prefix emit."
The
.B e770
.RB ( SHA1BASE64CUSTBASE64MD5 )
type emits four variants of the
.B "{MD5}" .base64 .digest
construction, with and without the trailing newline
and with and without the closing
.B =
padding character on the base64 output.
.P
.B "TRUNC families: two cuts per length."
The
.B TRUNC
types truncate an intermediate
.B hex
digest to
.B N
characters and hash the result. For each
.BR N ,
most of these types emit
.B two
digests \(em one taking the
.B leading
.B N
hex characters and one taking the
.B trailing
.B N
\(em so a single password yields roughly twice as many
candidate digests as there are values of
.BR N :
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(cut(H(pass), 0, N))      /* leading  */
sha1(cut(H(pass), \-N, N))     /* trailing */
.ft
.DE
.B N
counts down from the full hex width of the inner digest
to the lower bound given in each type row; at
.B N
= the full width the two cuts coincide and only one
distinct digest results.
The value of
.B N
is reported in the salt field of mdxfind output, and for
the salt-bearing types as
.RB \(lq "salt N" \(rq.
.P
The following emit the
.B leading
cut only:
.BR e635 " (SHA1WRLTRUNC),"
.BR e638 " (SHA1SHA512TRUNC1SALT),"
.BR e644 " (SHA1MD6TRUNC),"
.BR e653 " (SHA1WRLUCTRUNC),"
.BR e657 " (SHA1MD6CAPTRUNC),"
.BR e672 " (SHA1WRLUCTRUNCSALT),"
.BR e683 " (SHA1MD5TRUNCSALT),"
and
.BR e739 " (SHA1SHA1TRUNCSALT)."
.P
.B "Single-byte salt (1SALT)."
The
.B 1SALT
types
.BR e638 " (SHA1SHA512TRUNC1SALT)"
and
.BR e639 " (SHA1SHA1PASS-TRUNC1SALT)"
do
.B not
take an external salt. The
.B salt
shown in their expressions is a single byte that mdxfind
generates internally, iterated across the printable range
.BR 0x09 \(en 0x7f
(119 values), in the same manner as
.B e348
and
.BR e349 .
Any
.B \-s
salt file is ignored. The byte is reported in the salt
field as
.RB \(lq "N 0xKK" \(rq
for
.B e638
and
.RB \(lq 0xKK \(rq
for
.BR e639 ,
which has no truncated digest of its own: it hashes the
complete
.B e601
.RB ( SHA1PASS-TRUNC )
digest with the byte appended.
.P
.B "BCRYPT256 (e577): two constructions, selected by the salt form."
The type row gives the
.B $2a$
form. A
.B $2k$
salt selects a
.B different
algorithm entirely \(em Python
.B passlib.hash.bcrypt_sha256
\(em and mdxfind computes both:
.DS
.ft CW
$2a$ $2b$ $2y$   bcrypt(sha256(pass), salt)
$2k$             bcrypt(base64(hmac_sha256(key=salt, msg=pass)), salt)
.ft
.DE
The
.B $2k$
form is
.B 59
characters, not 60:
.DS
.ft CW
$2k$ <cost> $ <21 salt chars> <31 checksum chars>
.ft
.DE
Only 21 of the 22 salt characters are stored. The 22nd carries just two
significant bits, so it is dropped and recovered by trying the four
canonical values
.BR .euO ,
which is why a single
.B $2k$
salt yields four candidate digests. Accordingly
.B \-z
emits
.B five
hashes per password for this type: one
.B $2a$
and four
.BR $2k$ .
.P
Source for the
.B $2k$
form is KoreLogic's own CMIYC 2024 write-up, which names
.B passlib.hash.bcrypt_sha256
directly; samples are archived under
.BR contest/history/2024 .
Those samples carry no plaintexts, so the construction was confirmed instead
against vectors generated independently of both tools.
.P
.B "HUM families: two placements, and an inner iteration."
The seven
.B HUM
types append a short line-ending sequence
.B S
to the hex of an inner digest and hash the result. Two things about them are
not visible in the type row.
.P
First, each emits
.B two
digests per
.RB ( S ", " N )
pair \(em the separator is placed
.B both
after and before the hex:
.DS
.ft CW
OUTER(hex(inner) . S)      /* append  */
OUTER(S . hex(inner))      /* prepend */
.ft
.DE
.B S
runs over eight values: empty, a space, and the line-ending combinations
\fB\e r\fP, \fB\e n\fP, \fB\e r\e n\fP, \fB\e n\e r\fP,
\fB\e r\e r\fP and \fB\e n\e n\fP.
.P
Second,
.B N
is an
.B "inner iteration index" ,
not a repeat count for
.BR S .
mdxfind computes
.B inner
once from the password and then re-hashes the
.B hex
of that digest, emitting at every round:
.DS
.ft CW
inner(0) = INNER(pass)
inner(k) = INNER(hex(inner(k-1)))
.ft
.DE
The salt field records the pair as
.RB \(lq "<sephex>- x N" \(rq,
so a salt of
.B "0a- x 2"
means separator
.BR 0x0a ,
with the inner hash applied twice. Reading
.B N
as a repeat count for the separator bytes does not reproduce any emitted
digest.
.P
Two expressions in this family were previously recorded inverted and are
corrected above:
.B e575
and
.B e576
hash the UTF-16LE expansion with
.BR MD4 ,
so the outer hash is the NTLM-shaped one their outer-first names already
imply, not the inner
.BR md5 / sha1 .
.B e378
places
.B S
inside the
.BR sha1 ,
before the outer
.BR md5 .
.H 3 "Note [25]"
.B "SHA1-CUSTOMUSERSALT (e535)."
The username is carried
.I inside
the stored hash, after the base64 field, and is hashed together
with a fixed 15-byte pepper baked into mdxfind.
Writing
.B A
for the SHA-1 of the password:
.DS
.ft CW
A = sha1(pass)
B = sha1(A)
C = sha1(user . "r2chYO214w>1a32" . B)
C = C ^ A
emit(base64(C) . ":" . user)
.ft
.DE
The XOR covers the whole 20-byte digest: mdxfind writes it as
.B "md5buf.v[0..2] ^= curin.v[0..2]"
and
.B v
is an array of
.BR "unsigned long long" ,
so three elements span 24 bytes, not 12.
.H 3 "Note [26]"
.B "MD5AM (e432) and MD5AM2 (e433)."
Both read the
.B \-u
channel, not
.BR \-s ,
and both fold the password to lower case before hashing.
A single
.B \-u
entry supplies
.I two
fields, split at the first colon; the rows above spell the first field
.B user
and the second
.B salt2
only because the language has no operator that selects a field of a slot.
Writing the entry as
.BR "u1:u2" :
.DS
.ft CW
e432  md5(u1 . "::" . lower(pass))
e433  md5(u1 . "::" . lower(pass) . ":" . u2 . ":73@^bhhs&#@&^@8@*$")
.ft
.DE
The doubled colon is not a separator between two supplied values: mdxfind
stages the lowered password in a buffer whose two preceding bytes are both
set to
.BR ':' ,
then copies the first user field in behind them.
For e433 the second field is appended after the password together with the
colon that delimited it, followed by the 19-byte literal shown, which begins
with a colon of its own.
An entry with no colon leaves e433 with the same value as e432, since the
append is guarded on the delimiter being present.
.H 3 "Note [27]"
.B "SHA1MD5SALTPASSPEPPER (e607)."
The type name is what settles the intended form.
In this family a
.B SALT
token immediately after
.B SHA1
marks a salt in the outer concatenation, as in
.B SHA1SALTMD5PASSPEPPER
(e602),
.BR "sha1(salt . md5(pass) . pepper)" .
e607 carries no such token, so its only salt is the one inside the inner
digest, and the outer hash covers the digest and the pepper alone.
The sibling types in the same block confirm it: they anchor the digest at a
fixed point in the buffer, place the salt before it and the pepper after it,
and hash the whole span.
.H 3 "Note [28]"
.B "MD4UTF16UC (e570)."
The expression is the first iteration only.
Under
.B \-i
this type re-applies the ENCODING as well as the digest, so the chain is
.DS
.ft CW
x(n+1) = upper(md4(utf16le(x(n))))
.ft
.DE
and not the uppercase digest chain that
.B md4_uc^N
would give.
Verified against all five iterations of
.BR "\-i 5" .
The
.B ^N
operator iterates a single function rather than a composite, so no
output-role form expresses this and the row is deliberately left describing
x01 alone.
Worth contrasting with
.B "SHA1UCUTF16LE (e730)"
which looks like the same shape but does
.I not
re-encode: its chain is plain
.BR "sha1_uc^N(utf16le(pass))" ,
and it is written that way.
The two are not interchangeable.
.H 3 "Note [29]"
.B "MD4UTF16 (e496), NTLM (e369) and NTLMH (e786)."
All three rows give the same primary form,
.BR "md4(utf16le(pass))" ,
and the three compile to identical bytecode.
That identity is real only in the degenerate ASCII single-variant case.
The types differ in four independent ways, none of which an hx expression
carries.
.P
.B Emissions.
Each password produces a different number of candidates: e496 one, e786 two,
e369 three.
This holds even for pure ASCII, where the values coincide but the
multiplicity does not, so the three are distinguishable in output at every
input.
.P
.B Encoding.
This is the paragraph most often read backwards, so it is worth being exact
about who invented what.
.P
Microsoft invented this hash when their wide character
.I was
UTF\-16LE, and they believed that sufficient to represent every language.
The operating system mapped the user's code page into UTF\-16LE using its own
conversion tables, as an OS service.  It specifically did
.I not
zero\-extend.
.P
.B e369
is therefore the specification type: it applies three real encodings through
.BR iconv ,
UTF\-8, CP1251 and CP1252, and dedups identical results with a
.BR memcmp .
.B e786
zero\-extends instead, which is what
.I hashcat
does, and it is a
.I hack
\(em it exists because hashcat cannot convert a code page at all, having no
tables and treating candidate bytes as opaque.  It is a compatibility type,
not a specification type.
.P
The persistent confusion is this: CP1252's code points mostly sit below
U+0100, so a 1:1 look of the mapping is mistaken for a zero fill.  It is not.
The two agree only over 0xA0\-0xFF, where the code points happen to equal the
byte values; the whole of 0x80\-0x9F diverges.  CP1252 0x80 is U+20AC, so
Windows hashes
.B "AC 20"
where a zero fill hashes
.BR "80 00" .
That band holds the euro sign, the curly quotes, the en and em dashes and the
trademark sign, so
.B e786
cannot reproduce a genuine Windows digest for any password containing one of
them, while
.B e369
can.
.P
For ASCII every variant collapses to one digest, which is why the three test
identical; they separate as soon as a byte above 0x7f appears.
With the password
.B $HEX[636166c3a9]
\(em the letters c, a, f followed by the two UTF\-8 bytes of an e\-acute \(em
e369 emits three distinct digests and e786 emits two.
.P
.B e496
assumes its input is UTF\-8 and cannot accept a code\-page encoding, where
Windows natively could.  Windows held UTF\-16LE internally and performed the
code\-page conversion on
.IR output ,
so the code page is recorded nowhere in the resulting text and the recipient
is simply required to know it.  That is what
.BR encforce (1)
exists to untangle, and the reason the output side is moving to UTF\-8: e496
converts back to UTF\-8 for the solved output, so the answer carries its own
encoding.
.P
.B Iteration.
e496 honours
.BR \-i ,
producing x01, x02, x03 and so on.
e369 and e786 do not: both call the check at a fixed iteration of one, so
they emit x01 only however high
.B \-i
is set, and the repeated x01 lines are the separate encoding variants rather
than successive rounds.
.H 3 "Note [30]"
.B "HMAC-WRL (e228)."
The row gives the construction, the full Whirlpool HMAC of 64 bytes. A
comparison against
.B \-z
output can show fewer hex characters than that, and the difference is NOT a
truncation in the algorithm. The row must not describe one.
.P
Match width in mdxfind is a property of the MATCHER, not of the hash. Two
things set it. The HMAC cases assign
.B hmac_len
beside
.BR hmac_type ,
and the shared
.B HMAC_start
label hands it to
.BR checkhashkey ,
where it bounds the span searched; across the family it is twice the digest
width in bytes, MD5 32, SHA-1 40, RIPEMD-320 80, SHA-512 128, while
.B JOB_HMAC_WRL
carries 64. The digest itself is always computed in full.
.P
On top of that,
.B Minhashlen
tracks the SHORTEST hash in the loaded list \(em it starts at 256 and each
hash read lowers it \(em and
.B checkhashkey
slides a window of that width along the computed digest. When the input
contains short hashes mdxfind therefore reduces the size of the match on
purpose, so that a short hash is found wherever it sits inside a longer
digest. Overlap detection is the point.
.P
This is why an oracle comparison against
.B \-z
must compare the CONSTRUCTION and treat a width difference as expected. An
earlier revision of this row encoded the observed width as
.B "cut(hmac_wrl(pass, user), 0, 64)"
and was wrong to: that asserts the algorithm yields 32 bytes, which it does
not.
.H 3 "Note [31]"
.B "MANGOS (e540), and why John dynamic_35 must not be mapped to it."
The row is correct and uppercases BOTH operands. It is the inner SHA-1 of the
WoW and ManGOS SRP6 credential, the value that is then salted and exponentiated
to make the verifier, so it is a component rather than a whole stored
credential.
.P
John the Ripper carries
.B dynamic_35
commented
.BR "sha1(uc($u).:.$p) (ManGOS)" ,
and the resemblance is misleading. That row does NOT uppercase the password:
its function list appends the userid, the colon constant, then
.B DynamicFunc__append_keys
unmodified. Checked against John's own three preload vectors for
.BR ELEV__CHARS/test1 ,
.B U1/thatsworking
and
.BR NINECHARS/test3 ,
all of which reproduce only with the password left as typed. For
.B SOLAR
and
.B PASSWORD
this row gives ed03969e..., while John's construction gives eae82942..., so the
two are different algorithms that happen to share a name in a comment.
.P
The real ManGOS construction is the one implemented here, and John implements
it too, in
.B wow_srp_fmt_plug.c
rather than in dynamic_35: that file states U and P are both upper case and
calls
.B enc_strupper
on each before hashing. Its inner stage reproduces this row exactly.
.P
Neither
.B dynamic_35
nor
.BR dynamic_36 ,
which is the same shape with no uppercasing at all, has any equivalent in this
catalog. Their absence from the John map is therefore correct and deliberate,
not an omission waiting to be filled. Mapping either onto e540 would attribute
cracks to the wrong algorithm, and the failure would be silent, since both
produce a well-formed 40-character digest for the same inputs.
.\" DEDUP_OK e369: NTLM, MD4UTF16 (e496) and NTLMH (e786) share the primary
.\" form md4(utf16le(pass)) by design, and are identical ONLY in the
.\" degenerate ASCII single-variant case. They differ in emission count
.\" (1 / 3 / 2), in encoding treatment (e786 reproduces hashcat's incorrect
.\" UTF-8 to UTF-16 mapping on purpose), and in whether -i is honoured
.\" (e496 yes, e369 and e786 x01 only) -- see Note [29]. Reviewed 2026-08-28.
.H 3 "Note [32]"
.B "RVARY (e1012)."
IBM DES-ECB of the EBCDIC password, keyed by the RACF key
schedule derived from that same password: each byte is mapped through the EBCDIC table,
XORed with 0x55, shifted left one bit and given odd parity. The password is
truncated to 8 characters, the plaintext padded with EBCDIC space 0x40 and the
key with 0x2a. It differs from
.B "e881 RACF"
only in that the plaintext is the password rather than the username, and is
.I not
uppercased. No hx expression is given because the key derivation is not a
composable primitive; note also that
.B racf_encrypt()
uppercases its user operand, so
.B "upper(racf_encrypt(pass, pass))"
agrees with the published test vectors only because both use uppercase
passwords, and diverges for any lowercase password.
.H 3 "Note [33]"
.B "SAMSUNGSHA1 (e867)."
Samsung Android. 1024 rounds of SHA-1 over the previous digest, the decimal
round index, the password and the salt:
.DS
.ft CW
round 0 : sha1("0" . pass . salt)
round i : sha1(prev . str(i) . pass . salt)
.ft
.DE
.B str(i)
is the decimal representation of the round index and
.B prev
the digest of the preceding round.
.H 3 "Note [34]"
.B "ISCSI-CHAP (e888)."
iSCSI CHAP.
.DS
.ft CW
md5(id_byte . pass . fromhex(challenge))
.ft
.DE
The salt field packs two values, the challenge and the identifier, as
.BR challenge_hex : id_hex .
.H 3 "Note [35]"
.B "SIPHASH (e902)."
SipHash-2-4. The row gives the computation. In the stored form the
.B c
and
.B d
round counts precede the key.
.H 3 "Note [36]"
.B "SAP-PASSCODE (e924) and SAP-PASSCODE5 (e925)."
SAP CODVN F/G. The digest of the first SHA-1 selects both a length and an
offset into a fixed magic string, and the second SHA-1 runs over the password,
that slice and the uppercased salt:
.DS
.ft CW
h = sha1(pass . upper(salt))
n = 32 + sum(h[0..9] mod 6)
o = sum(h[10..19] and 7)
    sha1(pass . magic[o..o+n] . upper(salt))
.ft
.DE
e925 truncates that result and pads it back out to the stored width:
.DS
.ft CW
cut(SAP-PASSCODE, 0, 20) . "00000000000000000000"
.ft
.DE
RFC_READ_TABLE returns the first 10 bytes. The type name says 5, but the
width is 10.
.H 3 "Note [37]"
.B "SSPR-SHA256 (e978) and SSPR-SHA512 (e979)."
SSPR. The salt is the base64 TEXT as stored, not its decoded bytes; the
iteration count N comes from the stored format; and the chaining is on the
binary digest rather than its hex.
.H 3 "Note [38]"
.B "AS400SSHA1 (e986)."
IBM AS/400 Salted SHA-1. The salt is the userid, uppercased and padded with
spaces to 10 characters, and both operands are converted to UTF-16 big-endian:
.DS
.ft CW
sha1(utf16be(cut(upper(salt) . "          ", 0, 10))
     . utf16be(pass))
.ft
.DE
The padding literal between the quotes is ten spaces.
.H 3 "Note [39]"
.B "SYMFONY256 (e992)."
Symfony.
.B sha512(pass)
followed by 9999 rounds of SHA-512 over the 128-character hex of the previous
digest. The last 4999 of those rounds append the salt.
.H 3 "Note [40]"
.B "ORACLE11 (e1015)."
Oracle 11g. The digest is
.B "upper(sha1(pass . fromhex(salt)))"
\(em the same value
.B SHA1PASSHEXSALT
(e834, hashcat mode 112) computes. The two differ only in stored form, and that
difference is the reason both exist. e834 takes the digest and the salt as two
separate fields; e1015 owns the form Oracle actually stores, a single
60-character field holding the 40-hex digest immediately followed by the 20-hex
salt. No other type in the catalogue has a 30-byte digest, so that width
identifies the format on its own, with no wrapper. Found lines are reported in
the same concatenated form, so a result can be handed straight back to Oracle.
.PF "'Appendix B' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.H 3 "Note [41]"
.B "SAPCODVNH256 (e1016), SAPCODVNH384 (e1017)."
SAP CODVN H in its SHA-256 and SHA-384 flavours. The stored form is
.B "{x-isSHA256, ITER}"
or
.B "{x-isSHA384, ITER}"
followed by base64 of the digest immediately followed by the salt. The
computation is
.B "h = H(pass . salt)"
for the first round and
.B "h = H(pass . h)"
for each of the remaining
.B ITER-1
rounds, with the password truncated at 40 characters. The iteration count is
carried in the hash rather than fixed by the type, so it is a runtime parameter
and these rows are documentation rather than a compilable expression.
.P
These complete the family: e904 SAPCODVNH is the SHA-1 flavour and e984
SAPCODVNH512 the SHA-512 one. All four of John the Ripper's
.B saph
SHA-256 and SHA-384 test vectors were reproduced by an independent
implementation before either type was written.
.H 3 "Note [42]"
.B "H3C (e1019)."
H3C, HPE and Huawei device passwords. The stored form is
.B "$h$6$<salt>$<base64>"
and the digest is
.B "sha512(pass . NUL . salt . pass . NUL)"
\(em the password is hashed INCLUDING its terminating NUL, twice, with the salt
between the two copies. The 64-byte digest is stored base64-encoded. Verified
against all three of John the Ripper's own
.B h3c
test vectors, base64 exact.
.H 3 "Note [43]"
.B "ARGON2MD5 (e1020)."
vBulletin 6. Argon2 over the MD5 of the password rather than the password
itself, the direct successor to what
.B BCRYPTMD5
(e451) models for vBulletin 5. The inner digest is the 32-character lowercase
HEX, not the 16 raw bytes, matching e451 and vBulletin's own convention. The
stored form is ordinary Argon2 MCF and carries no marker distinguishing it from
e987 ARGON2, so a line can be offered to either type and only the correct one
verifies.
.P
Neither John the Ripper nor hashcat ships a vector for this scheme. The
registered one was produced by
.B argon2-cffi ,
a different implementation from the argon2 library linked here, so the check is
across implementations rather than self-generated.
.H 3 "Note [44]"
.B "DRAGONFLY3-32, DRAGONFLY3-64, DRAGONFLY4-32, DRAGONFLY4-64 (e1021\(en e1024)."
DragonFly BSD's own crypt schemes. Both set up like
.B SHA256CRYPT
(e512) and
.B SHA512CRYPT
(e513) but return after a
.I single
digest of pass . magic . salt \(em there is no round loop, which makes these
among the cheapest salted types in the catalogue.
.P
The magic is the format tag
.I including
its NUL terminator. On 64-bit builds its length was taken as 8 rather than 4, so
four adjacent rodata bytes ride along after the NUL: "sha5" for $3$ and "/etc"
for $4$. That is the bug the variant names refer to, and both variants are in
the wild. Because the NUL is interior to the hashed string, neither can be
expressed as an ordinary salted type with a textual salt.
.P
The digest is emitted with the sha256crypt/sha512crypt transposition, but $4$
encodes only 62 of the 64 digest bytes \(em bytes 62 and 63 never appear in the
stored form, so a $4$ hash cannot be decoded back to a full digest. mdxfind and
hashpipe therefore encode forward and compare strings.
.P
The stored forms of the 32- and 64-bit variants are
.I identical
\(em nothing in a $3$ or $4$ line says which produced it. A line is loaded into
both types of its pair and only the correct one verifies. All 24 of John the
Ripper's vectors were reproduced by an independent implementation before this
code was written, and
.B "mdxfind \-z"
regenerates John's published strings byte for byte.
.H 3 "Note [45]"
.B "GOST12256CRYPT (e1025), GOST94CRYPT (e1026)."
The two remaining members of the GOST crypt family, siblings of
.B GOST12512CRYPT
(e994). All three are the Drepper schedule \(em the same one
.B SHA256CRYPT
(e512) and
.B SHA512CRYPT
(e513) use \(em with the SHA replaced by a GOST digest, defaulting to 5000
rounds and accepting an explicit rounds= field.
.P
e1025 substitutes Streebog-256 and e1026 substitutes GOST R 34.11-94. Both
produce a 32-byte digest and so use the sha256crypt transposition and a
43-character stored hash, where e994 uses the sha512crypt transposition and 86.
.P
e1026 uses the TEST parameter S-box, not CryptoPro \(em i.e. the same set as
.B GOST
(e13), not
.B GOST-CRYPTO
(e14). John the Ripper's gost94hash carries a
.B john_gost_cryptopro_init
override but leaves it commented out, and the bundled gosthash used here is the
test-parameter build, so the two agree.
.P
Streebog output is byte-reversed relative to the published GOST vectors by the
bundled Saarinen implementation, so e1025 reverses each digest exactly as e994
does. All of John's vectors for both types verify, and mdxfind -z output cracks
back.
.H 3 "Note [46]"
.B "SUNMD5 (e1027)."
Solaris crypt. The initial digest is md5(pass . salt); thereafter 4096 plus the
rounds= value are run, each computing md5 of the previous digest, optionally a
fixed 1517-byte phrase, and the decimal round number in ASCII.
.P
Whether the phrase is included is decided per round by a coin flip derived from
bits of the previous digest \(em two seven-bit indices are assembled from the
digest bytes and the XOR of the bits they address gives the flip. Roughly half
the rounds therefore hash an extra 1517 bytes, so the cost is data dependent and
varies with the candidate rather than being fixed by the rounds= field alone.
.P
The phrase is 1516 characters of Hamlet's soliloquy
.I plus
its terminating NUL: Sun's implementation passed sizeof(), so 1517 bytes are
hashed, and dropping the NUL yields a plausible but wrong digest for every
candidate.
.P
The salt is everything before the LAST dollar sign. That definition also covers
the variant whose salt ends in a dollar sign and so shows a doubled separator in
the stored form, with no special case. The digest is emitted with the md5crypt
transposition, 22 characters. All 8 of John the Ripper's vectors verify,
including its 120-character password, and mdxfind -z output cracks back.
.H 3 "Note [47]"
.B "SHA1MD5USER (e308), MD5CAPMD5USER (e354), MD5CAPMD5MD5USER (e355), MD5MD5MD5USER (e356)."
Four entry points into one loop in mdxfind, reached by fallthrough, differing
only in how the intermediate hex is built: one md5 or two, with the leading hex
character capitalised or not. The capitalisation of the
.I first
hex happens in the e355 pre-block, so a reading that caps only the last md5
agrees with mdxfind whenever that character is a digit and disagrees otherwise
\(em about five times in eight.
.P
For every user, two digests are emitted: one over hex . user and one over
hex . ":" . user. Both are iterated, each over its own hex. Registering the
pair so that only one of them seeds the iteration chain leaves the type
complete at its base depth and exactly half strength at every depth below,
which is how the defect presented before it was found.
.P
Earlier revisions of this page gave these expressions as md5(md5(user)) and the
like, with no password term at all. That was a transcription error; the
password is the inner operand and the username is appended.
.H 3 "Note [48]"
.B "MD5DSALT (e408)."
The two salts are reported in a single field, concatenated with no separator,
so a line carrying the salt
.B Kpa
does not say whether that is
.B Kp
then
.BR a ,
or
.B K
then
.BR pa .
Anything consuming this type has to try each split; mdxfind bounds each salt at
three characters, so at most three positions need testing. A reader that
assumes an even split agrees only when the two salts are the same length.
.H 3 "Note [49]"
.B "Two UTF-16LE conventions."
mdxfind turns a password into UTF-16LE in two different ways, and which one a
type uses is not visible from its expression.
.P
The first is a byte expansion: each input byte is followed by a NUL, so the
bytes are treated as Latin-1. The second is a real UTF-8 to UTF-16LE
conversion through iconv, opened with //IGNORE so that input which is not valid
UTF-8 has the bad sequences dropped rather than failing the conversion.
.P
They agree for ASCII and differ for everything else.
.B DCC2 ,
.B AZURESYNC ,
.B KRB5PA23
and
.B KRB5TGS23
use the byte expansion;
.B MSONLINE
and plain
.B NTLM
use iconv. An implementation that picks one convention for all of them will
verify ASCII passwords and quietly fail every other one.
.H 3 "Note [50]"
.B "CRYPTOPPLEGACY (e1028), CRYPTOPPDEFAULT (e1029)."
Crypto++'s
.B DataEncryptor
framework. The stored value is 8 bytes of salt in the clear followed by CBC
ciphertext over
.B "keycheck || plaintext || PKCS#7" ,
where
.B keycheck
is
.B SHA1(key\ .\ salt)
truncated to the cipher's block size. The key and IV come from
.B Mash(key\ .\ salt) ,
an iterated SHA construction over a growing buffer.
.P
e1028 is DES-EDE2 with SHA-1 over 200 iterations, an 8-byte block and an 8-byte
key-check. e1029 is AES-128 with SHA-256 over 2500 iterations, a 16-byte block
and a 16-byte key-check. Stored length is a multiple of the block size plus the
8-byte salt, and at least 24 bytes.
.P
.B "These have no hx expression."
Every other entry in this table computes a value to be compared with what was
stored. Here the stored value is the ciphertext and the comparison is internal:
the first block is decrypted and checked against the key-check. Expressing that
needs a verify predicate rather than a digest.
.P
.B "The key is a salt whose cardinality is 1."
One key covers every record on an installation. Supply it with
.B \-s ,
or with
.B \-S
after
.B \-M ,
exactly as for any other salt. The candidate stream is the key list, so the
wordlist does not affect the result. Output is
.B "record:sitekey:plaintext" ,
the ordinary salted shape, with the decrypted plaintext in the last field so
.B getpass
and the ledger receive a password.
.P
.B "Naming."
e1028 is the construction Crypto++ calls
.B LegacyEncryptor .
A binary whose RTTI names
.B DefaultEncryptor
and whose build predates the rename is e1028, not e1029; the
.B DefaultEncryptor
name now belongs to the AES and SHA-256 type, which is e1029.
.P
e1028 is confirmed against a vendor DLL. e1029 is verified against upstream
Crypto++ and has no field sample, so a hit on it identifies the construction and
not any particular application.
.H 1 "Appendix B: Intrinsic Function Reference"
.PF "'Appendix B' \\\\nP'Waffle'"
.P
This appendix documents every built-in function available in hx,
with its default output format and a concrete example using the
password
.ft CW
password
.ft
and, where applicable, the salt
.ft CW
salt
.ft
(the four bytes 0x73, 0x61, 0x6c, 0x74).
.P
All examples can be reproduced with:
.DS
.ft CW
echo password | hashpipe -X 'expression'
.ft
.DE
.H 2 "Output Format Suffixes"
.P
Every digest-producing function (hash, HMAC, KDF, cipher)
supports role suffixes that control the output encoding.
The bare name uses the function's default role; the
suffixed form overrides it:
.TS
center box;
l l l.
Suffix	Role	Description
_
(none)	default	Function's canonical output (usually hex)
_bin	ROLE_BIN	Raw binary bytes
_hex	ROLE_HEX	Lowercase hexadecimal
_uc	ROLE_UC	Uppercase hexadecimal
_b64	ROLE_B64	RFC 4648 base64 with = padding
_mcf	ROLE_MCF	Modular Crypt Format (crypt-family only)
.TE
.TB "Output format suffixes"
.P
Not every suffix is valid for every function.
The supported combinations are:
.BL
.LI
.B "ROLES_DIGEST"
(hash, HMAC, KDF) \(em supports _bin, _hex, _b64, _uc.
Default is _hex.
.LI
.B "ROLES_CRYPT"
(bcrypt, yescrypt) \(em supports _bin, _hex, _b64,
.I and
_mcf.
Default is _mcf.
.LI
.B "ROLES_NONE"
(string transforms, encoders) \(em no suffix
accepted.
The function returns a string directly.
.LE
.P
To illustrate why suffixes matter, consider
.B bcrypt .
The default (MCF) output is the familiar crypt string
that encodes the algorithm, cost, salt, and hash
together:
.DS
.ft CW
.ps 9
$ echo password | hashpipe -X 'bcrypt(pass, fromhex("6162636465666768696a6b6c6d6e6f70"), 5)'
$2b$05$WUHhXETkX0fnYkrqZU3ta.xKSDhHZGYxrgOUh/C3/852ZM1RjPsCa
.ps
.ft
.DE
.P
This is self-contained: any bcrypt implementation can
verify the password against this string.
The alternative forms strip the metadata:
.DS
.ft CW
.ps 9
bcrypt_hex(...)  ccc5058c96c86b3b624168c113907eef86cedd3951b847
bcrypt_b64(...)  xKSDhHZGYxrgOUh/C3/852ZM1RjPsCa
bcrypt_bin(...)  (23 raw bytes)
.ps
.ft
.DE
.P
These contain only the 23-byte hash output.
Without the salt and cost factor, they are
.I useless
for verification \(em you cannot reconstruct the
bcrypt input from the raw hash alone.
The _hex and _bin forms exist for compositions where
bcrypt's output feeds into another function
(e.g., a site-specific wrapper that computes
.ft CW
sha256(bcrypt_bin(pass, salt, cost))
.ft R
), not for standalone storage.
.H 2 "Cryptographic Hash Functions"
.P
These are the core message digest functions.
Each takes a single byte-string argument and returns
the digest.
Default output is lowercase hex.
.P
All hash functions registered by hashpipe (approximately
1000 types) are available as hx intrinsics.
The table below lists the most commonly used primitives.
.TS
center box;
l l l.
Function	Digest Size	Example Output (pass = "password")
_
md4(pass)	128 bit	8a9d093f14f8701df17732b2bb182c74
md5(pass)	128 bit	5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99
sha1(pass)	160 bit	5baa61e4c9b93f3f0682250b6cf8331b7ee68fd8
sha224(pass)	224 bit	d63dc919e201d7bc4c825630d2cf25fdc93d4b2f0d46706d29038d01
sha256(pass)	256 bit	5e884898da28047151d0e56f8dc6292773603d0d6aabbdd62a11ef721d1542d8
sha384(pass)	384 bit	a8b64babd8acb29e42e9a9862daa23bb\(dg
sha512(pass)	512 bit	b109f3bbbc244eb82441917ed06d618b\(dg
.TE
.TB "Core hash functions (default hex output)"
.P
\(dg Output truncated for display; full output is 96 or 128 hex characters.
.P
The same value in all three encodings:
.DS
.ft CW
.ps 9
md5(pass)      5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99
md5_b64(pass)  X03MO1qnZdYdgyfeuILPmQ==
md5_bin(pass)  (16 raw bytes)
md5_hex(pass)  5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99
.ps
.ft
.DE
.P
Note that
.B md5(pass)
and
.B md5_hex(pass)
produce identical output: the bare name defaults to hex
for all digest functions.
The explicit _hex suffix is available for clarity in
complex expressions.
.P
Additional hash functions available include (but are not
limited to): md2, sha0, gost, gost_crypto, rmd128,
rmd160, tiger, tth, ed2k, aich, snefru128, snefru256,
hav128, hav160, hav192, hav224, hav256, whirlpool,
sha3_224, sha3_256, sha3_384, sha3_512, keccak224,
keccak256, keccak384, keccak512, blake2b_256,
blake2b_512, blake2s_128, blake2s_256, skein256,
skein512, skein1024, streebog256, streebog512,
panama, sm3, and all BLAKE variants.
Every hash type known to hashpipe is automatically
registered as an hx function.
.H 2 "HMAC Functions"
.P
HMAC functions take two arguments: the key and the
message.
The key is typically the password; the message is
typically the salt or a derived value.
.TS
center box;
l l l.
Function	Digest Size	Example (key = "password", msg = "salt")
_
hmac_md5(pass, salt)	128 bit	dda73d480bbd86b0da55b1480e7d0b30
hmac_sha1(pass, salt)	160 bit	c1d0e06998305903ac76f589bbd6d4b61a670ba6
hmac_sha256(pass, salt)	256 bit	84ec44c7d6fc41917953a1dafca3c7d7\(dg
hmac_sha512(pass, salt)	512 bit	(128 hex characters)
.TE
.TB "HMAC functions (default hex output)"
.P
\(dg Truncated for display.
HMAC functions support _bin, _hex, and _b64 suffixes.
.H 2 "Key Derivation Functions"
.P
KDFs take additional parameters for iteration count and
output length.
.TS
center box;
l l l.
Function	Parameters	Default Role
_
pbkdf2_sha1(pass, salt, iter, dklen)	4	hex
pbkdf2_sha256(pass, salt, iter, dklen)	4	hex
pbkdf2_sha512(pass, salt, iter, dklen)	4	hex
pbkdf2_md5(pass, salt, iter, dklen)	4	hex
pbkdf1_sha1(pass, salt, iter, dklen)	4	hex
bcrypt(pass, salt, cost)	3	mcf
yescrypt(pass, salt)	2	mcf
scrypt(pass, salt, N, r, p, dklen)	6	hex
argon2id(pass, salt, t, m, p, dklen)	6	hex
argon2i(pass, salt, t, m, p, dklen)	6	hex
argon2d(pass, salt, t, m, p, dklen)	6	hex
argon2(pass, salt, t, m, p, dklen)	6	hex (alias for argon2id)
pomelo(pass, salt, t_cost, m_cost)	4	hex
.TE
.TB "Key derivation functions"
.P
Example:
.DS
.ft CW
.ps 9
$ echo password123 | hashpipe -X 'pbkdf2_sha256(pass, fromhex("73616c74"), 1000, 32)'
632c2812e46d4604102ba7618e9d6d7d2f8128f6266b4a03264d2a0460b7dcb3

$ echo password123 | hashpipe -X 'pbkdf1_sha1(pass, fromhex("73616c74"), 1000, 20)'
c5baaad10eb03e9602037df936ccf3f5e8d9b4e8

$ echo password123 | hashpipe -X 'pomelo(pass, fromhex("73616c74"), 2, 3)'
79bfb3e29e6be446560e1c52a06690734ef28634f662a31ff11b9973514c6be0
.ps
.ft
.DE
.P
.B pomelo()
is the PHC (Password Hashing Competition) submission
POMELO v2 by Hongjun Wu.
The
.B t_cost
and
.B m_cost
parameters control time and memory hardness;
mdxfind uses t_cost=2, m_cost=3 by default.
.P
The crypt-family KDFs (bcrypt, yescrypt) default to MCF
output, which includes the algorithm identifier, cost
parameters, and salt in a single self-verifying string.
See the bcrypt example in the Output Format Suffixes
section above.
.H 2 "Crypt-Family Functions"
.P
These functions implement the Drepper-pattern crypt
algorithms (and related MCF-producing functions).
Default output is MCF (Modular Crypt Format);
all support _hex, _b64, _bin suffixes to extract
the raw hash digest.
.TS
center box;
l l l.
Function	Parameters	MCF Prefix
_
md5crypt(pass, salt)	2	$1$
apr1(pass, salt)	2	$apr1$
sha256crypt(pass, salt [, rounds])	2\(en3	$5$
sha512crypt(pass, salt [, rounds])	2\(en3	$6$
sm3crypt(pass, salt [, rounds])	2\(en3	$sm3$
gost12_512crypt(pass, salt [, rounds])	2\(en3	$gost12512hash$
descrypt(pass, salt)	2	(13-char)
phpass(pass, salt, log2count)	3	$H$
.TE
.TB "Crypt-family functions (default MCF output)"
.P
Examples (pass = "password123", salt = "salt"):
.DS
.ft CW
.ps 9
md5crypt(pass, salt)
  $1$salt$/3NHsNrNmNbOO90IOW9dw/

sha256crypt(pass, salt)
  $5$salt$XLWJeVAbH3W178D74IkDX6.dlQByMbBzb/KgZVmw486

sha512crypt(pass, salt)
  $6$salt$cevuzTZ/QBjzuZG0/ebEeedm...

sm3crypt(pass, salt)
  $sm3$salt$8ZfxTI2EU8nZHmzZjhleiDc7S/jAjfTrsRQv1bXp2l5

gost12_512crypt(pass, salt)
  $gost12512hash$salt$6ORzFltcwpBfn0QJn9XWHk.7q3Wi2...

descrypt(pass, "ab")
  abJnggxhB/yWI

phpass(pass, "saltsalt", 11)
  $H$9saltsaltngurp8A.5QxWvhTtS2J2I0
.ps
.ft
.DE
.P
The _hex suffix extracts the raw hash digest as hex,
stripping the MCF wrapper:
.DS
.ft CW
.ps 9
md5crypt_hex(pass, salt)
  4d676a50a49a91767622b87c417872da

md5crypt_b64(pass, salt)
  TWdqUKSakXZ2Irh8QXhy2g==
.ps
.ft
.DE
.P
For crypt functions with a
.B rounds
parameter, the default is 5000.
Custom round counts are embedded in the MCF output:
.DS
.ft CW
.ps 9
sha256crypt(pass, salt, 10000)
  $5$rounds=10000$salt$2L6cVpxJfG/se8rqocEPWP1W9Lh4ma7rj9nLxMmPb0A
.ps
.ft
.DE
.H 2 "Kerberos and Protocol Functions"
.P
These functions implement the key derivation steps
used by Kerberos authentication protocols.
.TS
center box;
l l l.
Function	Args	Description
_
rc4_hmac_md5(pass)	1	Kerberos etype 23 key (HMAC-MD5 of NTLM hash)
aes128_cts_hmac_sha1(pass, salt)	2	Kerberos etype 17 key (PBKDF2-SHA1, 16 bytes)
aes256_cts_hmac_sha1(pass, salt)	2	Kerberos etype 18 key (PBKDF2-SHA1, 32 bytes)
.TE
.TB "Kerberos and protocol functions"
.P
Examples (pass = "password123"):
.DS
.ft CW
.ps 9
rc4_hmac_md5(pass)
  358bd5d2c85ea4cddfa181278c361fdd

aes128_cts_hmac_sha1(pass, salt)
  06ca0b78b558933164fc00313fe02a10

aes256_cts_hmac_sha1(pass, salt)
  06ca0b78b558933164fc00313fe02a10\\
  52738b98c736e77da8f3080a84f5c3bc
.ps
.ft
.DE
.P
.B rc4_hmac_md5()
computes the Kerberos etype 23 base key:
HMAC-MD5(MD4(UTF16LE(pass)), usage=1).
The AES functions compute the string-to-key
derivation: PBKDF2-SHA1(pass, salt, 4096, keylen).
These are the password-derived keys used in KRB5PA23,
KRB5TGS23, KRB5PA-17, KRB5PA-18, KRB5DB17, and
KRB5DB18 hash types.
